<example>
  <title>Exploring Huntington–Hill with a Table</title>
  <p>
    Three states have the populations shown below.  Each state has already
    received one seat.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tabular>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>State</cell>
        <cell>Population</cell>
        <cell>Current seats (<m>n</m>)</cell>
        <cell>Priority
          (<m>\frac{P}{\sqrt{n(n+1)}}</m>)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>A</cell>
        <cell>10,000,000</cell>
        <cell>3</cell>
        <cell>\fillin{}</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>B</cell>
        <cell>6,000,000</cell>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell>\fillin{}</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>C</cell>
        <cell>3,000,000</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>\fillin{}</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>Compute the priority value for each state.</li>
      <li>Which state has the largest priority?</li>
      <li>Which state receives the next seat?</li>
      <li>Repeat for the fifth seat.</li>
      <li>Find a modified divisor that produces the same apportionment with Huntington-Hill as a divisor method.</li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</example>

<remark>
  <title>Geometric Interpretation</title>
  <p>
    The table above reflects a geometric partition of quota space.
    For Huntington–Hill, each possible integer seat allocation corresponds to a
    region bounded by surfaces where
    <m>q = \sqrt{n(n+1)}</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a state’s quota crosses one of these geometric-mean boundaries, the
    allocation changes by one seat.  In the priority formulation, these same
    boundaries appear as comparisons of priority values between states.
  </p>
  <p>
    From this perspective, Huntington–Hill assigns each population vector to
    the integer seat allocation whose region contains it.  The method’s use of
    the geometric mean ensures that these regions balance relative
    over- and under-representation.
  </p>
</remark>